DESCRIPTION:Ruby on Rails - Intro Tutorial Workshop will show you how to cr
 eate a simple real estate web app using Rails 3.\n\nYou will learn how to s
 tart and configure Rails 3 app. Then\, we will discuss the basics of Rails 
 framework. In about an hour and half\, we are going to create the back-end 
 of the real estate system.\n\nThis workshop is intended for people who has 
 basic or no previous knowledge of Ruby on Rails. \n\nBefore joining the wor
 kshop\, you must download the following software:\n\nhttps://www.virtualbox
 .org/\nhttp://www.turnkeylinux.org/rails\nhttp://filezilla-project.org/
LAST-MODIFIED:20221122T084631Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Ruby on Rails - Intro Tutorial Workshop
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:HackLab.TO
DTSTART:20150315T180000Z
DTEND:20150315T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260512T040225Z
UID:ibhcom6785dtt7u7kh2u9mv5ok@google.com
CREATED:20150205T021720Z
DESCRIPTION:http://www.meetup.com/DIYbio-Toronto/events/221014261/\n\nJoin 
 DIYbio Toronto and Hacklab for a taste-tripping party!  Based on tablets of
  Synsepalum ducificum\, commonly known the miracle fruit\, you'll be eating
  lemons like they are candy!   The fruit has been used by people since at l
 east the 18th century to make acidic foods taste sweet.\n\nIn addition to a
  tablet of the berry\, your ticket includes a variety of foods and beverage
 s for you to sample\, including:\n\nLemons and Limes\nOranges\nGrapefruit\n
 Onions\nSour Apples\nLemonade\nOlives\nStrawberries\nCheese (Blue)\nBeer (m
 aybe Guinness\, for tasting only)\nWine\nSalt and Vinegar Chips\nPickles\na
 pple cider vinegar\nChocolate\nSour Candy\nCherry Flavored Soda\n\n\nFor yo
 u science buffs (let's face it: all of us)\, Wired has an interesting descr
 iption of how it works:\n\n"The researchers used a system of cultured cells
  that let them test human taste receptors at various levels of acidity and 
 alkalinity. They found that miraculin binds strongly to the sweet taste bud
 s (specifically hT1R2-hT1R3) but — unlike sugar or aspartame — doesn’t acti
 vate them at a neutral pH. Introduce acid\, though\, and the protein shifts
  shape in a way that “turns on” the taste bud\, creating a sensation of ult
 ra-sweet that drowns out the sour.\n\nWhen the sour\, acidic food is swallo
 wed\, the miraculin returns to its old inactive shape and remains firmly bo
 und to the sweet receptor for about an hour or so\, lying in wait for anoth
 er acidic treat."\n  -- http://www.wired.com/2011/09/sweet-sour-berry/\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20150312T185401Z
LOCATION:Hacklab Classroom\, 1266 Queen Street West #12\, Toronto
SEQUENCE:2
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:DIYbio Taste Tripping Party
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
