DTEND:20080914T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260512T040225Z
UID:lvfbbi207mf2kn62tbsffit5eg@google.com
CREATED:20080909T171349Z
DESCRIPTION:Microcontrollers such as PIC and Atmel offer designers incredib
 le \nopportunities for making very sophisticated embedded projects. No matt
 er \nwhich kind of chip you're using\, the major hardware challenge of \nmi
 crocontroller-based projects is interfacing to the outside world. This \nwo
 rkshop explores some of the common interfacing methods used for both \ninpu
 t and output with some demonstrations and time for experimenting and \ndisc
 ussion.                                                                    
                                  \n\nBring your laptop and your favourite M
 CUs. Some sample circuits will be \navailable on-site for experimenting\, a
 nd there will be a supply run to Creatron to get controllers if you don't h
 ave one.\n\n\nAbout the Presenter:\n\nAndrew Kilpatrick has worked designin
 g electronics for over 15 \nyears. He has developed devices in the areas of
  lighting\, music\, \naudio\, video\, system supervisory and control. He cu
 rrently works as \nthe Lead System Developer at Onestop Media Group and als
 o develops his \nown projects in his spare time. He lives and works in Toro
 nto.\n                                   \nTopic highlights:\n\n- AC mains 
 detecting and control \n- fan\, motor\, solenoid and relay control\n- switc
 h sensing tips\n- analog inputs: pots and voltages\n- LED control \n\nNotes
 \, circuits\, etc. will be posted on Andrew's website after the presentatio
 n. 
LAST-MODIFIED:20221122T084509Z
LOCATION:hacklab.to
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Microcontroller Interfacing Ideas - Workshop
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
